"AWS Glue":{"security":["Restrict AWS Glue producers, consumers, catalogs, workgroups, indexes, schemas and destinations independently; analytics access does not automatically protect source data.","Encrypt AWS Glue source, intermediate, checkpoint, result and index data and constrain cross-account roles and KMS use.","Audit AWS Glue schema, connector, query, stream, job, catalog and destination changes while keeping sensitive payloads out of verbose logs."],"optimization":["Tune AWS Glue from its native partition, shard, task, worker, checkpoint, query, refresh or throughput model; aggregate capacity can hide a single hot partition or slow stage.","Use AWS Glue-appropriate filtering, projection, compression, batching, partitioning and retention to reduce bytes processed before scaling infrastructure.","Separate AWS Glue interactive, streaming, scheduled and recovery workloads so bursty consumers do not starve latency-sensitive paths."],"cost":["Price AWS Glue from its documented scan, ingestion, processing, request, capacity, storage, retention or visualization units.","For AWS Glue, data volume, query shape, retries, idle workers, checkpoint retention and cross-Region transfer can dominate the service charge.","Include S3, Glue, Lambda, KMS, OpenSearch, Redshift, dashboards and network costs created by the AWS Glue pipeline."],"watchPoints":["AWS Glue is one stage in a data path; validate schema, freshness, checkpoints, duplicates, skew, permissions and downstream contracts.","A successful AWS Glue query, job or ingest can still produce an incorrect result when late data, stale metadata or partial failures are ignored.","Watch AWS Glue lag/backlog, throttling, failed records, quota limits and freshness SLOs—not only CPU or request count."]},
"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams":{"security":["Use stream and consumer IAM policies separately, and restrict enhanced fan-out and resharding permissions to operators that need them.","Encrypt records with a customer-managed KMS key when stream data requires independent key control; protect consumer applications and checkpoints too.","Use CloudTrail for stream, shard and retention changes, and avoid putting secrets into records because downstream consumers may replicate them."],"optimization":["Partition keys determine shard distribution; a hot key can throttle one shard while total stream capacity appears underused.","Choose on-demand or provisioned capacity from traffic volatility, and use aggregation/batching to improve producer efficiency.","Enhanced fan-out changes consumer isolation and throughput behavior; select it for latency-sensitive consumers instead of enabling it universally."],"cost":["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams cost follows its documented service unit: include request/query/message/byte, capacity, storage, or seat dimensions rather than using a generic compute estimate.","The dominant driver for Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is workload-shaped: model volume, retention, retries, fan-out, idle capacity and cross-Region or cross-AZ transfer explicitly.","Include the adjacent services that Amazon Kinesis Data Streams causes to run, such as S3, KMS, Lambda, logs, data transfer, indexes, endpoints or downstream databases."],"watchPoints":["Kinesis Data Streams preserves records only for the configured retention period; it is not automatically a long-term data lake.","Ordering is per partition key, not global; resharding and consumer lag must be considered in replay designs.","Monitor iterator age, write/read throttles and expired records—the last one can silently turn lag into data loss."]},
