"AWS App Runner":{"security":["Apply least privilege to AWS App Runner launch, task, pod, function, image, job and deployment roles; separate runtime data access from control-plane mutation.","Keep AWS App Runner secrets, images, volumes and logs protected with encryption, private paths and controlled administrative entry points.","Audit AWS App Runner launch, image, scaling, deployment and runtime changes and scan artifacts before promotion."],"optimization":["Tune AWS App Runner from its native unit—vCPU/memory, task or pod density, image pull, job queue, instance family, concurrency or placement capacity—using measured utilization.","Use AWS App Runner health checks, immutable artifacts, autoscaling, rolling/blue-green strategies and graceful draining according to the workload’s failure domain.","Keep AWS App Runner stateless front ends, durable state and asynchronous workers separate so each scales from its own bottleneck."],"cost":["Model AWS App Runner runtime, capacity, request, image, storage, log and transfer dimensions together; a node or function price is not the full path.","For AWS App Runner, right-size CPU/memory and remove idle capacity only after checking startup, interruption, availability and downstream protection requirements.","Include load balancing, NAT, ECR storage/transfer, logs, attached storage and downstream calls created by AWS App Runner."],"watchPoints":["AWS App Runner scaling is not the same as application scaling; inspect queues, databases, connection pools, quotas and placement capacity.","A healthy AWS App Runner deployment or control plane does not prove the workload is serving a healthy dependency.","Watch AWS App Runner startup/image-pull latency, placement failures, throttling, health checks, retries, draining and version skew."]},
