"Amazon Redshift":{"security":["Restrict Amazon Redshift producers, consumers, catalogs, workgroups, indexes, schemas and destinations independently; analytics access does not automatically protect source data.","Encrypt Amazon Redshift source, intermediate, checkpoint, result and index data and constrain cross-account roles and KMS use.","Audit Amazon Redshift schema, connector, query, stream, job, catalog and destination changes while keeping sensitive payloads out of verbose logs."],"optimization":["Tune Amazon Redshift from its native partition, shard, task, worker, checkpoint, query, refresh or throughput model; aggregate capacity can hide a single hot partition or slow stage.","Use Amazon Redshift-appropriate filtering, projection, compression, batching, partitioning and retention to reduce bytes processed before scaling infrastructure.","Separate Amazon Redshift interactive, streaming, scheduled and recovery workloads so bursty consumers do not starve latency-sensitive paths."],"cost":["Price Amazon Redshift from its documented scan, ingestion, processing, request, capacity, storage, retention or visualization units.","For Amazon Redshift, data volume, query shape, retries, idle workers, checkpoint retention and cross-Region transfer can dominate the service charge.","Include S3, Glue, Lambda, KMS, OpenSearch, Redshift, dashboards and network costs created by the Amazon Redshift pipeline."],"watchPoints":["Amazon Redshift is one stage in a data path; validate schema, freshness, checkpoints, duplicates, skew, permissions and downstream contracts.","A successful Amazon Redshift query, job or ingest can still produce an incorrect result when late data, stale metadata or partial failures are ignored.","Watch Amazon Redshift lag/backlog, throttling, failed records, quota limits and freshness SLOs—not only CPU or request count."]},
