"Amazon API Gateway":{"security":["Use Amazon API Gateway's route, listener, endpoint, DNS and identity controls as separate boundaries; reachability does not equal authorization.","Prefer private paths where the Amazon API Gateway design supports them, protect TLS/certificates/origin credentials and restrict direct bypass routes.","Audit Amazon API Gateway configuration and retain flow, DNS, access or edge logs with redaction and ownership appropriate to the traffic data."],"optimization":["Design Amazon API Gateway around its native routing, health-check, connection, cache, endpoint and failure-domain semantics; remove unnecessary hops before adding capacity.","Choose the Amazon API Gateway connectivity or routing primitive from ownership, transitivity, protocol, latency and scale requirements rather than using a familiar default.","Tune Amazon API Gateway using target health, cache behavior, connection reuse, MTU/TLS and cross-AZ/Region placement measurements."],"cost":["Model Amazon API Gateway from its requests, connections, bytes, endpoints, hours, health checks, acceleration, transfer and attached inspection/logging services.","Path placement matters: cross-AZ, cross-Region, NAT and internet egress can dominate the Amazon API Gateway front-door charge.","Remove idle listeners/endpoints/routes and right-size logging or edge execution only after the availability and investigation requirements are clear."],"watchPoints":["Amazon API Gateway controls a particular traffic path; it does not automatically provide application identity, backend authorization or data correctness.","Watch the Amazon API Gateway-specific failure signal—DNS convergence, route propagation, target health, connection exhaustion, cache freshness, throttling or endpoint status.","Validate failover from the client and backend perspective; control-plane success for Amazon API Gateway is not proof that real traffic uses the intended path."]},
