"AWS Artifact":{"security":["For AWS Artifact, separate administration, detection, data access and response permissions; a security finding or key/secret control must not be alterable by the workload it protects.","Use AWS Artifact's native policies, resource scope, encryption and retention settings with explicit account/Region ownership; avoid broad organization-wide exceptions.","Send AWS Artifact evidence to a controlled destination and audit changes to rules, findings, keys, identities, certificates or protected resources."],"optimization":["Tune AWS Artifact around its documented control boundary—policy evaluation, inspection, discovery, scanning, classification, key use or finding workflow—rather than enabling every control at maximum scope.","Use suppression, sampling, exclusions, aggregation and automation only when the false-positive and coverage trade-off is measured for AWS Artifact.","Automate enrichment and low-risk response for AWS Artifact, but preserve approval and rollback for access-changing or destructive actions."],"cost":["Price AWS Artifact from its native coverage, requests, keys, protected resources, findings, scans, logs or storage dimensions.","Reduce duplicate scans, noisy findings, unnecessary retention and unneeded cross-account copies while preserving the required detection or audit coverage.","Include KMS, CloudTrail, Security Hub/SIEM, NAT, inspection endpoints and remediation compute when AWS Artifact triggers them."],"watchPoints":["AWS Artifact is a specific security control, not a replacement for the other layers; identify what it detects, blocks, encrypts or authorizes and what it cannot see.","Watch coverage gaps, policy shadowing, exception expiry, key/secret rotation, finding quality and service quotas for AWS Artifact.","Test failure behavior for AWS Artifact: denied access, unavailable key, expired certificate, missing log delivery or a false positive must be observable and recoverable."]},
