"Amazon Detective":{"security":["Restrict behavior-graph access because investigations expose account, identity, network and resource relationships; separate investigators from administrators.","Protect cross-account member invitations and exported investigation data, and ensure GuardDuty source data has the intended retention and access controls.","Audit graph membership, administrator changes and investigator access."],"optimization":["Use Detective for relationship and timeline analysis after a finding, not as a high-volume prevention or alerting pipeline.","Organize investigator workflows around finding IDs, entities and time windows so analysis stays focused on the relevant behavior graph.","Export only the evidence needed for the case; avoid duplicating full graph data into broad-access stores."],"cost":["Amazon Detective cost follows its documented service unit: include request/query/message/byte, capacity, storage, or seat dimensions rather than using a generic compute estimate.","The dominant driver for Amazon Detective is workload-shaped: model volume, retention, retries, fan-out, idle capacity and cross-Region or cross-AZ transfer explicitly.","Include the adjacent services that Amazon Detective causes to run, such as S3, KMS, Lambda, logs, data transfer, indexes, endpoints or downstream databases."],"watchPoints":["Detective helps investigate relationships; it does not block an attack or prove root cause by itself.","Investigation quality depends on source telemetry and the selected time window.","Cross-account graph membership and Region scope determine whether the investigator can see the entities involved."]},
