"Amazon Bedrock":{"security":["Use model-invocation, customization, guardrail, knowledge-base and agent permissions separately; do not grant every application access to every foundation model.","Control model inputs, outputs, prompts, training data, guardrails, KMS keys and logging destinations according to data classification.","Audit model access, inference profiles, provisioned throughput, custom model and guardrail changes; retain evaluation evidence for production releases."],"optimization":["Choose the model and API from quality, latency, context, modality and cost requirements; use the Converse or Responses pattern consistently for the application.","Use prompt caching, batching, asynchronous invocation, model routing or inference profiles where the workload supports them.","Separate retrieval, orchestration, generation and evaluation so a quality problem is not hidden by scaling the endpoint."],"cost":["Amazon Bedrock cost follows its documented service unit: include request/query/message/byte, capacity, storage, or seat dimensions rather than using a generic compute estimate.","The dominant driver for Amazon Bedrock is workload-shaped: model volume, retention, retries, fan-out, idle capacity and cross-Region or cross-AZ transfer explicitly.","Include the adjacent services that Amazon Bedrock causes to run, such as S3, KMS, Lambda, logs, data transfer, indexes, endpoints or downstream databases."],"watchPoints":["Bedrock provides managed access to models; model behavior, quotas, Region availability and guardrail coverage vary by model and feature.","Token/context limits and cross-Region inference affect latency, data residency and cost.","A guardrail is a safety layer, not a complete authorization or business-rule engine."]},
