"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases":{"security":["Protect data-source connectors, ingestion roles, embeddings, vector stores and retrieval results; retrieval permissions must align with the source document’s authorization model.","Encrypt source, vector and generated data with controlled KMS keys and keep secrets for connectors in Secrets Manager.","Audit ingestion, chunking, embedding-model, vector-store and retrieval-configuration changes; preserve document provenance."],"optimization":["Choose chunking, parsing, embedding model, vector store and retrieval strategy from document structure and query behavior; larger chunks are not automatically better.","Use metadata filters and reranking to reduce irrelevant context before generation, and schedule ingestion from actual source-change frequency.","Separate ingestion from query serving and monitor index freshness, retrieval latency and top-k quality."],"cost":["Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases cost follows its documented service unit: include request/query/message/byte, capacity, storage, or seat dimensions rather than using a generic compute estimate.","The dominant driver for Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases is workload-shaped: model volume, retention, retries, fan-out, idle capacity and cross-Region or cross-AZ transfer explicitly.","Include the adjacent services that Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases causes to run, such as S3, KMS, Lambda, logs, data transfer, indexes, endpoints or downstream databases."],"watchPoints":["A knowledge base can return a relevant-looking answer from stale or unauthorized chunks if synchronization and metadata filters are wrong.","Changing an embedding model or chunking strategy usually requires a controlled re-ingestion and evaluation.","The vector store’s own scaling, backup and access model remains part of the RAG security boundary."]},
