"Amazon Q Business":{"security":["Use IAM Identity Center or IAM and source-system ACL synchronization so responses are permission-aware; indexing a document does not grant every user access.","Protect connectors, index capacity, plugin actions, application data accessors and chat logs; task actions need narrowly scoped execution roles.","Audit connector syncs, web experiences, plugins, subscriptions and access changes, and retain citations/evidence for sensitive workflows."],"optimization":["Select connectors and sync cadence from source volatility and user need; continuous ingestion of unchanged content wastes capacity and can create stale conflicts.","Use metadata filters, retriever configuration and source permissions to improve answer quality before increasing model or index capacity.","Keep read-only Q&A separate from action-taking plugins and require confirmation for external side effects."],"cost":["Amazon Q Business cost follows its documented service unit: include request/query/message/byte, capacity, storage, or seat dimensions rather than using a generic compute estimate.","The dominant driver for Amazon Q Business is workload-shaped: model volume, retention, retries, fan-out, idle capacity and cross-Region or cross-AZ transfer explicitly.","Include the adjacent services that Amazon Q Business causes to run, such as S3, KMS, Lambda, logs, data transfer, indexes, endpoints or downstream databases."],"watchPoints":["Q Business answers from enterprise sources and can cite them, but bad ACL synchronization can produce either leakage or empty answers.","Index capacity and user subscriptions are separate pricing dimensions; both belong in the estimate.","Connector failures, deleted documents and conflicting source versions affect freshness and trustworthiness."]},
